module Darklang.Stdlib

/// Wait here for a value that may still be a read in flight, rather than where it is first
/// used. Calling a function does not wait for its arguments, so this is the one way to choose
/// the wait point: a builtin always gets values. (`Exec.await` is the one for a handle from
/// `Exec.spawn`.)
let await(x: 'a): 'a =
  Builtin.runtimeForce(x)


/// <param fn> applied to <param x>, without waiting for <param x> here when it is a read still
/// in flight (an `HttpClient.get`, say): the answer is then a read in flight too, and
/// <param fn> runs when <param x> arrives. So a function of a response does not make the
/// request wait where it was sent. A value that is not in flight is applied at once.
///
/// What runs when the read arrives runs after your program has moved on, so <param fn> may
/// only compute: one that prints, writes or sends fails, where the result is waited for. To
/// take an effect, wait first (<fn Stdlib.await>) and call <param fn> on the value.
let whenReady(x: 'a, fn: 'a -> 'b): 'b =
  Builtin.runtimeWhenReady(x, fn)
